  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nCloud cover continues to gradually decrease from west to east \nacross the region this afternoon as a weak low-pressure system \npositioned to our south loses its influence on eastern Idaho's \nweather. Lingering moisture and cloudiness remain anchored over \nfar eastern and southeastern zones. High-resolution convective- \nallowing models (CAMs) indicate sufficient instability to \ngenerate isolated showers and perhaps a stray thunderstorm later\nthis afternoon and evening, generally focused east of the I-15 \ncorridor. Precipitation chances will diminish rapidly late this \nevening, becoming non-existent by midnight as atmospheric drying\ntakes hold. Overnight lows will remain seasonably cool under \nclearing skies.\n\nA broad, tranquil weather regime takes over across eastern \nIdaho for the first half of the workweek. With the departing \nsystem well to the south and high pressure beginning to \norganize to the west, precipitation chances remain near zero. \nDaytime high temperatures Tuesday and Wednesday will hover right\naround late- September climatological norms, topping out in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s across lower-elevation valleys.\n\nConfidence remains very high in a prolonged dry and unseasonably\nwarm weather pattern developing Thursday and extending into the\nfirst week of October. Global model ensembles are in strong\nagreement that an upper-level ridge will build directly over the\nIntermountain West on Thursday and remain anchored over the\nregion through the weekend. Rising 500 mb heights and subsidence\nwill drive a noticeable warming trend, pushing daytime maximum\ntemperatures 7 to 10 degrees above seasonal averages. Lower-\nelevation valleys will widespread reach the mid-to-upper 70s\nstarting Thursday, with localized spots likely topping 80\u00b0F\nduring peak diurnal heating. Given the strength and positioning\nof the ridge, measurable precipitation is highly unlikely\nthrough the upcoming weekend and likely through much of the\nfollowing week as well.",
